      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=6\\cos  x\\log  x,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})^{′}(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}})+(6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}})^{′} \\\\  & =(- 6 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}})+(6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})(\\frac{1}{x \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{6 \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}} + \\frac{6 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{x \\ln{\\left(10 \\right)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
